Instead of sorting every item one by one, this version counts how many times each value appears. The map gives the values in increasing order. They are pushed from largest to smallest so the smallest value ends up at the top.

Repeated values are handled by storing their count. This method is useful when the stack can contain duplicates.

```java
import java.util.Map;
import java.util.Stack;
import java.util.TreeMap;

class Solution {
    public void sortStack(Stack<Integer> st) {
        TreeMap<Integer, Integer> counts = new TreeMap<>();

        while (!st.isEmpty()) {
            int value = st.pop();
            counts.put(value, counts.getOrDefault(value, 0) + 1);
        }

        for (Map.Entry<Integer, Integer> entry : counts.descendingMap().entrySet()) {
            for (int count = 0; count < entry.getValue(); count++) {
                st.push(entry.getKey());
            }
        }
    }
}
```

Each map update costs O(log n), so the total time is O(n log n). The map needs O(n) space in the worst case. The original stack is modified.

A recursive version keeps the stack sorted while elements are added back. The top of the stack is kept as the smallest value. After removing one value, the rest is sorted first, then that value is inserted in the right place.

Empty and one-item stacks need no special work beyond the base condition.

```java
import java.util.Stack;

class Solution {
    public void sortStack(Stack<Integer> st) {
        if (st.size() <= 1) {
            return;
        }

        int value = st.pop();
        sortStack(st);
        insertInOrder(st, value);
    }

    private void insertInOrder(Stack<Integer> st, int value) {
        if (st.isEmpty() || st.peek() >= value) {
            st.push(value);
            return;
        }

        int topValue = st.pop();
        insertInOrder(st, value);
        st.push(topValue);
    }
}
```

Each value can move through the stack many times, so the time cost is O(n^2). The recursive calls use O(n) extra space. The input stack is changed directly.

This uses a simple selection method. It finds the smallest remaining value, removes it from the array stack, and stores it in a separate list. The collected values are ascending, so they are pushed back in reverse order to make the smallest one the top item.

For values 3, 1, 2, the selected list is 1, 2, 3. Pushing 3, then 2, then 1 leaves 1 at the top.

```javascript
class Solution {
    sortStack(stack) {
        const selected = [];

        while (stack.length > 0) {
            let minimum = stack[0];

            for (const value of stack) {
                if (value < minimum) {
                    minimum = value;
                }
            }

            const buffer = [];
            let removed = false;

            while (stack.length > 0) {
                const value = stack.pop();
                if (!removed && value === minimum) {
                    removed = true;
                } else {
                    buffer.push(value);
                }
            }

            while (buffer.length > 0) {
                stack.push(buffer.pop());
            }

            selected.push(minimum);
        }

        for (let i = selected.length - 1; i >= 0; i--) {
            stack.push(selected[i]);
        }
    }
}
```

Finding and removing a minimum can take O(n), repeated n times, giving O(n^2) time. The temporary arrays use O(n) space. The input array is cleared and rebuilt.

The second stack is kept partly ordered while values are removed from the input. Larger values are moved aside until the current value can be placed. At the end, moving the second stack back gives the smallest value at the top.

For a quick dry run with 3, 1, 2, the helper stack becomes ordered with 3 at its top and 1 at its bottom. Transferring it back produces 1, 2, 3 from top to bottom.

An empty stack and a stack with one value are already sorted.

```csharp
using System.Collections.Generic;

class Solution
{
    public void sortStack(Stack<int> st)
    {
        Stack<int> ordered = new Stack<int>();

        while (st.Count > 0)
        {
            int value = st.Pop();

            while (ordered.Count > 0 && ordered.Peek() > value)
            {
                st.Push(ordered.Pop());
            }

            ordered.Push(value);

            while (st.Count > 0 && ordered.Count > 0)
            {
                ordered.Push(st.Pop());
            }
        }

        while (ordered.Count > 0)
        {
            st.Push(ordered.Pop());
        }
    }
}
```

The repeated moves make the time complexity O(n^2). The second stack uses O(n) extra space, and the supplied stack is updated in place.

Here the stack values are moved into a vector, sorted, and pushed back. Since pushing in reverse order leaves the smallest item on top, the final stack is sorted using normal stack operations.

This is short and easy to check, although it needs an extra array and does not keep the original stack contents untouched.

```cpp
#include <algorithm>
#include <stack>
#include <vector>

class Solution {
public:
    void sortStack(std::stack<int> &st) {
        std::vector<int> values;

        while (!st.empty()) {
            values.push_back(st.top());
            st.pop();
        }

        std::sort(values.begin(), values.end());

        for (int i = static_cast<int>(values.size()) - 1; i >= 0; --i) {
            st.push(values[i]);
        }
    }
};
```

Sorting takes O(n log n) time, and the vector uses O(n) space. The stack is emptied and rebuilt.

